                  "name": "sweep_interval_seconds",
                  "type": "int32",
                  "description": "sweep_interval_seconds is INERT as of 2026-09-27 and is read by nothing.\n\n It was the cadence of the STATE-ROOT sweep: `MailboxState.entries` was this\n service's own root in kernel, nothing else pruned it, and an unbounded one\n eventually reached its declared 1,000,000 bound and started refusing\n writes. There is no such root any more — the rows live in stratum, whose\n bound is disk and whose memory clock is `residency.idle_seconds` below, a\n per-type clock this service can neither observe nor set. A sweep from here\n would have nothing to sweep.\n\n WHAT WENT WITH IT: the 90-day `held_since_ms` floor. That age was the STATE\n ROOT's bound, not the record's, and no record-side clock replaces it —\n `residency.deleted_max_age_seconds` measures a purge from a\n `(spec.deleted_flag)` this record does not declare. So a mailbox entry is\n now kept for as long as stratum keeps it. See held_since_ms's comment and\n workers/mailbox-llmsettings-stratum-result.md; deliberately NOT closed here\n by inventing a delete path this migration was not asked for.\n\n (Until 2026-09-20 the sweep had a second half, reconciling the retired\n `ejectionable` marker. That half went first; this is the rest.)\n\n DECLARED, not deleted, for the same reason retrieved_grace_seconds below is:\n kernel holds mailbox's config, and removing a field kernel sets is a\n config-compatibility question this migration has no answer for. Both should\n be deleted and fields 1 and 2 reserved in a follow-up once nothing in kernel\n sets either.",
