{
  "name": "TenantUserLinkSide",
  "package": "kernel",
  "description": "TenantUserLinkSide is one half of a link: either one IdP login the calling\n broker has just verified, or one device that has just signed a WebAuthn\n assertion. Exactly one of the two shapes is set.",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "claims",
      "type": "message",
      "description": "claims are the verified IdP claims, the same shape MintUserToken carries —\n the IdP's own iss/sub/nonce under origin_*, copied verbatim out of the\n id_token the broker verified. origin_iss, origin_sub and origin_nonce are\n all required ON AN IdP SIDE: a side with no nonce cannot be bound to the\n other side. Absent on a device side, which carries no login at all.\n\n NOT assert_required, because a device side has no claims to carry. The\n handler requires exactly one of (claims, device_assertion) per side and\n refuses a side that sets both or neither — a check a field-level assertion\n cannot express."
    },
    {
      "name": "subject",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "subject is the SPIFFE user id this login resolves to, as\n MintUserTokenRequest.subject carries it. Required for an ordinary tenant\n side, where it is what names the user; IGNORED on a `people` side, which\n derives its user id from the verified claims and takes nothing from the\n request, and IGNORED on a device side, which derives its principal from the\n credential the assertion is signed by.",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 1024 bytes"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 1024,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "device_assertion",
      "type": "message",
      "description": "device_assertion is a WebAuthn assertion signed by the device's passkey.\n Setting it makes this a DEVICE side: the peer side must then be a `people`\n side, and the two are bound by the pair of checks the §3 design names —\n the peer's id_token `nonce` is this key's RFC 7638 thumbprint, and this\n assertion's challenge names that exact login. Neither half alone is\n accepted and nothing can be replayed onto a different device.",
      "message": {
        "name": "WebauthnAssertion",
        "package": "kernel",
        "description": "WebauthnAssertion is one WebAuthn (passkey) assertion, exactly as the\n authenticator produced it — the four fields of an\n AuthenticatorAssertionResponse, unaltered.\n\n NOTHING HERE IS PARSED BY A BROKER AND BELIEVED. Kernel recomputes the signed\n bytes itself (authenticator_data || SHA-256(client_data_json)), reads the\n challenge out of client_data_json itself, and verifies signature against the\n credential's own public key itself. A broker that rewrote any of these four\n fields produces a proof that fails, which is what keeps \"compromising the\n broker forges no identity\" (personalauth.proto's header) true of this path\n too.\n\n NO ATTESTATION OBJECT, DELIBERATELY. This is the desktop-passkey arm of the\n identity decision doc's §3 — the one whose device proof is explicitly the\n weaker one, because Windows/Linux have no App Attest / Play Integrity. The\n public key therefore arrives as an ordinary jwk.Jwk (the platform's key type\n everywhere else, including CreatePersonalPrincipal) and is trusted only as\n far as the nonce binding makes it trustworthy: the IdP's id_token carries\n that key's RFC 7638 thumbprint as its `nonce`, so a substituted key has no\n matching login. App Attest and Play Integrity are named as follow-ons, not\n stubbed: both need live verification against Apple's and Google's own\n services, and a mocked verifier would be a device proof nobody could trust.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "credential_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "credential_id is the authenticator's credential id, base64url without\n padding. It becomes PersonalCredential.credential_id.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 1024 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1024,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "authenticator_data",
            "type": "bytes",
            "description": "authenticator_data is the raw authenticatorData the authenticator signed.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 2048 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 2048,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "client_data_json",
            "type": "bytes",
            "description": "client_data_json is the raw clientDataJSON the authenticator signed over\n the SHA-256 of. Kernel parses it for `type` and `challenge`; both are\n checked, and the challenge is what binds this assertion to one IdP login.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 4096 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 4096,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "signature",
            "type": "bytes",
            "description": "signature is the authenticator's signature over\n authenticator_data || SHA-256(client_data_json), in the encoding the key's\n algorithm defines (DER for ECDSA, raw for Ed25519, PKCS#1 v1.5 for RSA).",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 2048 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 2048,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "trace_id",
            "type": "string",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "default_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 32768,
                "defaulted": true,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "default_bound"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "noop",
            "type": "bool"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "device_public_key",
      "type": "message",
      "description": "device_public_key is the public half of the passkey, as a jwk.Jwk. Kernel\n verifies device_assertion against THIS key on a first link — the same\n bootstrap CreatePersonalPrincipal has, where the key must come out of the\n request because no stored credential exists yet — and against the STORED\n key on every link after that. The request's copy is never consulted again\n once the credential row exists.",
      "message": {
        "name": "Jwk",
        "package": "jwk",
        "description": "Jwk represents a JSON Web Key as defined in RFC 7517.\n It contains the public key parameters for RSA (e, n), EC (crv, x, y), or OKP (crv, x) keys.\n JWK is used for key distribution and verification in JSON-based protocols like JWT.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "kty",
            "type": "enum",
            "description": "Kty is the key type parameter (RSA, EC, or OKP)",
            "enum_values": [
              {
                "name": "KEY_TYPE_RSA",
                "number": 1,
