{
  "name": "MailboxConfigState",
  "package": "mailbox",
  "description": "MailboxConfigState is the state boundary for mailbox service configuration.\n Auto-injected by tacplus from (spec.grpc_config) annotation.",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "config",
      "type": "message",
      "message": {
        "name": "Config",
        "package": "mailbox",
        "description": "Config is delivered by kernel at startup and on every change.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "sweep_interval_seconds",
            "type": "int32",
            "description": "sweep_interval_seconds is INERT as of 2026-09-27 and is read by nothing.\n\n It was the cadence of the STATE-ROOT sweep: `MailboxState.entries` was this\n service's own root in kernel, nothing else pruned it, and an unbounded one\n eventually reached its declared 1,000,000 bound and started refusing\n writes. There is no such root any more — the rows live in stratum, whose\n bound is disk and whose memory clock is `residency.idle_seconds` below, a\n per-type clock this service can neither observe nor set. A sweep from here\n would have nothing to sweep.\n\n WHAT WENT WITH IT: the 90-day `held_since_ms` floor. That age was the STATE\n ROOT's bound, not the record's, and no record-side clock replaces it —\n `residency.deleted_max_age_seconds` measures a purge from a\n `(spec.deleted_flag)` this record does not declare. So a mailbox entry is\n now kept for as long as stratum keeps it. See held_since_ms's comment and\n workers/mailbox-llmsettings-stratum-result.md; deliberately NOT closed here\n by inventing a delete path this migration was not asked for.\n\n (Until 2026-09-20 the sweep had a second half, reconciling the retired\n `ejectionable` marker. That half went first; this is the rest.)\n\n DECLARED, not deleted, for the same reason retrieved_grace_seconds below is:\n kernel holds mailbox's config, and removing a field kernel sets is a\n config-compatibility question this migration has no answer for. Both should\n be deleted and fields 1 and 2 reserved in a follow-up once nothing in kernel\n sets either.",
            "default": 300,
            "constraints": [
              "must be \u003e= 10",
              "must be \u003c= 86400"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_ge",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 10,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_le",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 86400,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "retrieved_grace_seconds",
            "type": "int32",
            "description": "retrieved_grace_seconds is INERT as of 2026-09-20 and is read by nothing.\n\n It had exactly one job: to defer the moment the sweep flipped a retrieved\n row's `ejectionable` from NEVER to WINDOW, so that a client which acks and\n then immediately re-reads still found the row resident. Both ends of that\n sentence are gone. The marker went with `spec.Ejectionable`, and residency\n is now a per-TYPE idle clock on the row's last touch — a grace period\n cannot shift it, because the retrieval write IS the touch.\n\n DECLARED, not deleted, on purpose: kernel holds mailbox's config, and\n removing the field is a config-compatibility question this migration has no\n answer for. It should be deleted and field 2 reserved in a follow-up once\n nothing in kernel sets it — see\n looper/schematic/workers/mailbox-ejectionable-migration-questions.md. The\n same now applies to sweep_interval_seconds above.",
            "default": 0,
            "constraints": [
              "must be \u003e= 0",
              "must be \u003c= 604800"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_ge",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 0,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_le",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 604800,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "trace_id",
            "type": "string",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "default_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 32768,
                "defaulted": true,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "default_bound"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "noop",
            "type": "bool"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "trace_id",
      "type": "string",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "noop",
      "type": "bool"
