{
  "name": "Message",
  "package": "messaging",
  "description": "Message is ONE DELIVERY: one message, to one recipient.\n\n It is NOT one row per submission. A submission naming three recipients\n becomes three `Message` rows sharing a `message_id`, differing in\n `delivery_id` and `recipient`, each independently consumable and\n independently ACKable. See the fan-out note at the top of this file for why\n EventBus cannot do that split itself.\n\n spec.record implies spec.event, so tacplus synthesizes the topic wrapper\n MessageBus with topic ref messaging.MessageBus.stream. Do NOT hand-write that\n wrapper.",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "delivery_id",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "delivery_id is the record key: \"\u003cmessage_id\u003e/\u003crecipient\u003e\". One per\n (message, recipient) pair, which is what makes the N publishes of one\n submission N separately-keyed records rather than N copies of one.\n\n Derived rather than random ON PURPOSE: the value a caller would need to\n reconstruct to ACK a delivery is exactly (which message, for whom), so\n deriving it means an agent that knows both never has to have been told the\n id. It is also what makes the fan-out replay-safe — a retried submission\n regenerates the same delivery ids rather than a second set of rows.",
      "required": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 512 bytes",
        "minimum size: 1"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_required",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "bool",
          "bool_value": true,
          "source": "annotation"
        },
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 512,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        },
        {
          "rule": "assert_min_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 1,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ],
      "record_key": true
    },
    {
      "name": "message_id",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "message_id groups the N deliveries of ONE submission. EQ-filterable,\n because \"every delivery of the message I sent\" is the question a sender\n asks, and it is the only way back from a delivery to its siblings.",
      "required": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 128 bytes"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_required",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "bool",
          "bool_value": true,
          "source": "annotation"
        },
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 128,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ],
      "filter_mode": "eq"
    },
    {
      "name": "sender",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "sender is who sent it — the authenticated principal subject bound by the\n (spec.principal) field on SendMessageRequest below, never a caller-written\n string.",
      "required": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 256 bytes"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_required",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "bool",
          "bool_value": true,
          "source": "annotation"
        },
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 256,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ],
      "filter_mode": "eq"
    },
    {
      "name": "recipient",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "recipient is THE recipient this delivery is for — one of `recipients`\n below, and the half of delivery_id that varies across the fan-out.\n\n It is also the ROW'S OWNING PRINCIPAL for the record query plane's c2s\n half. spiffe_id below is the record's declared owner but is the PUBLISHER —\n eventbus stamps messaging's own service identity onto it — so filtering a\n user's reads on that field would show every user nothing. `sender` is the\n other candidate and is the wrong one: a mailbox read is \"messages addressed\n to me\", so the field the generated egress filter holds a caller's reads to\n must be the addressee.\n\n The consequence is stated rather than hidden: a SENDER cannot read their own\n sent messages through this record's c2s plane, because only one field may\n carry (spec.store_principal) and this is it. Sent-items is a mailbox\n concern and the mailbox service is Phase 1 item 8, undesigned; inventing a\n second read path for it here would be inventing that service's shape.\n\n The EQ criterion is what carries the row filter into the plane's SIZE-ONLY\n RPCs: Get\u003cT\u003eCount returns a number over the handle's predicate and no rows,\n so no egress filter can narrow it. The generated c2s wrapper pins this\n criterion to the caller at Create\u003cT\u003eQuery and every later call on the\n handle inherits it.",
      "required": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 256 bytes"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_required",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "bool",
          "bool_value": true,
          "source": "annotation"
        },
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 256,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ],
      "filter_mode": "eq",
      "store_principal": true
    },
    {
      "name": "recipients",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "recipients is the full To: line — every recipient of the submission, the\n SAME list on all N deliveries. `recipient` above says which one this row is\n for; this says who else got it.\n\n Carrying it is not redundancy. Without it a recipient cannot see who else\n was addressed, which is information the sender chose to disclose by\n addressing them together — the same reason a To: header exists and a Bcc:\n one does not. (This file models no Bcc; nothing has asked for one.)\n\n Bounded at 256 to match Config.max_recipients' ceiling above.",
      "repeated": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 256 elements",
        "maximum element size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 256,
          "unit": "elements",
          "source": "annotation"
        },
        {
          "rule": "default_max_element_size",
          "target": "element",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "subject",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "subject is the message's subject line.",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 512 bytes"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 512,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "text",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "text is the message body. Free text, and nothing parses it — see ticket_id.",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 16384 bytes"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 16384,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "ticket_id",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "ticket_id OPTIONALLY references the ticket this message pertains to.\n\n A dedicated field, never a value embedded in `text`. Routing and status\n display have to associate a message with its ticket to act, and the\n standing rule is that anything that must be parsed to be acted on should\n have been a field. Optional because most messages pertain to no ticket, and\n EQ-filterable because \"every message about this ticket\" is the question the\n field exists to answer.\n\n This is the ONLY ticket-shaped thing on Message, and it is an opaque id.\n Ticket's own churn — advice sealing, decision chains, amend/reconsider —\n stays entirely inside Ticket. That is what keeps this type stable.",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 128 bytes"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 128,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ],
      "filter_mode": "eq"
    },
    {
      "name": "sent_at_ms",
      "type": "int64",
      "description": "sent_at_ms is the business clock — when the sender submitted — and the\n RANGE criterion, because \"my messages from last week\" is the question a\n mailbox asks.\n\n Identical across all N deliveries of one submission: they were one act.",
      "filter_mode": "range"
    },
    {
      "name": "spiffe_id",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "spiffe_id is the record owner. The name is LOAD-BEARING: eventbus's topic\n discovery matches the literal field name \"spiffe_id\" of string type when it\n decides which topics get publisher-identity stamping, so renaming this\n field silently turns the stamping off.\n\n It holds MESSAGING'S OWN service identity, not the sender's, because the\n publish is s2s from this service — that is the direct consequence of the\n SUPERSEDED ruling. `sender` above is where the human is.",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 1024 bytes"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 1024,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ],
      "filter_mode": "eq"
    },
    {
      "name": "spiffe_actor",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "spiffe_actor is the ORIGIN signal: the delegate that made the call, stamped\n by EventBus from the publishing token's `act` claim at publish time.\n\n   \"a message is marked as originating from the user directly or from the\n    user's Scotty acting on their behalf... A message sent by\n    Scotty-for-Dennis carries `act` populated; one Dennis sends directly\n    doesn't.\" — decisions/messaging-vs-ticketing-2026-08-31.md\n\n The service does not set it and must not: like spiffe_id above, a value\n written here would be a claim about identity that nothing verified.\n Provenance only — sys/spec.proto forbids wiring it into a principal.\n\n GAP, STATED NOT PAPERED OVER. That quote describes the pre-supersession\n model, where the user published to EventBus DIRECTLY and EventBus saw the\n user's own delegated token. Under the corrected model the publish is s2s\n from messaging, so the token EventBus stamps from is messaging's own\n service token and carries no `act` claim at all — this field will be empty\n in the ordinary case, including for a genuine Scotty-for-Dennis send. The\n annotation is declared anyway, and correctly: the seam is the right one and\n the field is where the value belongs the moment the delegated-publish path\n exists. Closing it needs an s2s publish that carries the caller's exchanged\n token, which the generated publisher does not offer today. Written up in\n workers/big8-p1-messaging-questions.md rather than hand-rolled around.",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 1024 bytes"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 1024,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
