        "description": "RuleNode is ONE node of one person's rule tree: a group, or a rule.\n\n ONE UNIFIED TYPE, per the ruling, rather than a Group record and a Rule\n record. A group and a rule are ordered against each other in the same list\n and precedence runs depth-first across both, so two types would mean two\n position spaces the runtime has to interleave, and interleaving them is\n exactly the ambiguity `position` exists to remove.\n\n ORDER IS PRECEDENCE. \"Rules are ordered inside their group; groups are\n ordered among their siblings. Earlier wins on conflict, depth-first.\" That is\n the deterministic tie-break the runtime needs and the lever the user already\n understands. Reordering is therefore a BEHAVIORAL CHANGE and gets the same\n treatment as a new rule — announced, silence-is-consent for self-affecting\n effects, bidirectional confirmation for DELEGATION.\n\n REWRITTEN IN PLACE. A record has no update RPC and is not getting one: rows\n reach the store by publish, so an UPDATE IS A REPUBLISH OF THE SAME node_id.\n (spec.record).mutability is left unset = MUTABILITY_CRUD, which is what every\n record in this repo does. MUTABILITY_APPEND_ONLY would be exactly wrong — it\n makes the generator refuse to emit any row-mutating path at all.\n\n NEVER HARD-DELETED. decisions/scotty-rules-and-filters-2026-09-19.md: \"a\n citation always resolves to the exact rule text that was in force, even after\n the user 'deleted' it.\" `deleted` below is the whole mechanism.\n\n spec.record implies spec.event, so tacplus synthesizes the topic wrapper\n RuleNodeBus with topic ref profile.RuleNodeBus.stream. Do NOT hand-write it.",
