        "description": "RuleMatch is Scotty's own DERIVED structured summary of what a rule is about.\n The ruling: \"match // Scotty-derived structured summary: labels, senders,\n rooms, time window\", and, decisively, \"`match` is Scotty's own derived\n summary, used only for cheap pre-filtering and for the 'what is being\n filtered' tap-through; it never decides.\"\n\n THE CONTRACT, stated here because every field below is meaningless without\n it and the design doc argues it at length:\n\n   1. IT IS A NECESSARY CONDITION, NEVER A SUFFICIENT ONE. A node whose match\n      fails may be skipped without reading it. A node whose match passes is\n      handed to the model WITH ITS `text`, and the text decides. There is no\n      path on which a rule fires because its match matched.\n   2. IT MAY OVER-SELECT AND MUST NEVER UNDER-SELECT. A match wider than the\n      sentence costs a few tokens. A match narrower than the sentence silently\n      breaks the rule, and the user has no way to see why — they can only read\n      the sentence, which still says what they think it says. Derivation is\n      conservative or it is wrong.\n   3. EMPTY MEANS \"NO PRE-FILTER AVAILABLE\", so the node is always a\n      candidate. That is the correct value whenever Scotty cannot summarize a\n      sentence, and it is the value a hand-written rule starts with.\n   4. WITHIN a field the values OR; ACROSS fields they AND. That is the whole\n      composition grammar and it is not going to grow one. No negation, no\n      wildcards, no nesting, no operators — each of those is the first step\n      into an expression language, and an expression language here would be a\n      second, weaker statement of the rule that can disagree with the\n      sentence. If a rule needs something this cannot say, the answer is an\n      empty match, not a richer schema.\n   5. IT IS DERIVED AND DISPOSABLE. Scotty recomputes it on any write and the\n      user never edits it. Nothing validates it against `text`, because\n      nothing could.\n\n NO (spec.query_field) ON ANYTHING IN HERE, and that is not an oversight: the\n runtime loads one user's rules — a bounded handful of rows, pinned by\n store_principal — and evaluates the match in process. Pushing these into\n store criteria would make the summary look like a query language, which is\n precisely what item 4 refuses.",
