      "description": "namespace is the row's owning namespace, held to the reader's claim by\n EXACT string equality. MANDATORY on a (spec.record): tacplus's\n ValidateRecordNamespaceAnnotations fails generation for any record\n declaring no such field, on the reasoning that the record nobody remembered\n to annotate is exactly the one that leaks. Left EMPTY by the producer on\n purpose — eventbus's PublishHandler stamps it from the publishing token's\n namespace claim and REFUSES a publish whose token carries none.\n\n IT IS THE `people` TENANT'S, AND IT IS NO LONGER THE MECHANISM. The\n publisher is this service's own identity and this service now lives in\n `people` (see the SERVICE CLASS note at the top), so the stamped claim is\n the `people` namespace — the same value on every row, for every person,\n which is exactly why it cannot be what keeps people apart. `private` above\n is what does that. The 2026-09-20 wording this replaces reasoned about a\n /system claim and about deriving a namespace per tenant; both are gone with\n the service class that produced them, and there is no namespace derivation\n anywhere in this file or in the handlers.\n\n The annotation is kept because it is MANDATORY and because it is a genuine\n backstop — a reader standing outside `people` is refused before `private`\n is even consulted, and decisions/identity-people-tenant-devices-private-2026-09-21.md\n §2 is what lets a caller standing in drpepper past it: the generated\n backstop accepts a row matching either the `namespace` claim or a `linked`\n claim. Per-company scoping of a RULE is still RuleNode.tenant_scope, which\n is a different axis from either.\n\n EQ-queryable because tacplus refuses (spec.record).c2s_read on a record\n whose namespace field is not: the client-facing plane pins this field to\n the caller's verified claim before the store is asked, and a pin needs a\n criterion to be pinned into. It stays uncallable by clients — the emitted\n request messages carry no