{
  "name": "GetMyTicketAuditRequest",
  "package": "scotty",
  "description": "GetMyTicketAuditRequest carries NO caller identity a caller may set.\n\n requester is the server-computed field §6's \"any predicate that would scope\n results to a caller is computed server-side from the verified Caller and is\n NEVER an LLM-writable tool argument\" names. It is the ordinary\n (spec.principal) marker, reused as-is per §8 — the generated MCP dispatch\n honours it the same way the gRPC wrapper does, and additionally OMITS it from\n the published tool schema and REFUSES a tool call that names it. So \"scoped\n to me\" is not a filter the model is trusted to pass; it is a value the model\n cannot express.\n\n NO (spec.namespace) FIELD, and that is a judgement, not an omission. The\n request-side marker's job is to bind a value the handler then STAMPS ONTO A\n ROW — that is why the four Create requests carry one. This rpc writes nothing.\n The tenant boundary it does need is on the READ side and is already generated\n from (spec.store_namespace) on the three row types this response carries: the\n dispatch resolves the caller's namespace claim itself, holds every returned\n row to it by EXACT equality, and aborts on a row it has to drop. A request\n field here would bind a value nothing reads.\n\n It is also, today, the difference between this tool having its generated\n regression tests and not having them — tacplus's own generated\n `\u003ctool\u003e_reaches_its_typed_handler` test builds a caller with a subject and no\n namespace claim, so any MCP tool declaring (spec.namespace) fails its own\n generated test. That is a tacplus gap, reported rather than worked around\n (workers/big8-p1-ticketing-retrofit-questions.md §2); it is NOT the reason\n the field is absent, but it does mean nothing is lost by its absence.",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "requester",
      "type": "string",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 256 bytes"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 256,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "chain_id",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "chain_id NARROWS the audit to one negotiation. Empty is every chain the\n caller opened. It is a real tool argument because it can only ever shrink\n an already-scoped result — a chain_id belonging to somebody else's\n negotiation returns nothing, because the requester predicate is applied\n first and is not this caller's to change.",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 64 bytes"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "assert_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 64,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "annotation"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "trace_id",
      "type": "string",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "noop",
      "type": "bool"
    }
  ]
}

