          },
          {
            "name": "namespace",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "namespace is the row's owning TENANT, and the read half of the isolation\n pair this retrofit adds. Its twin is (spec.namespace) on the Create request\n above: that one binds the field to the caller's namespace claim on the way\n IN (stamp when empty, accept when equal, INVALID_ARGUMENT when different);\n this one is what the generated wrapper filters egress rows against on the\n way OUT, by EXACT string equality — never the level-or-below containment\n the administrative namespace rule uses, because \"users and data always has\n to land in a leaf\".\n\n A row that has to be dropped by that filter ABORTS THE PROCESS rather than\n failing the one call. Reaching it means kernel handed this service another\n tenant's row, which is a leak the filter exists to make impossible, and the\n decision record settles that a crash is the cheaper failure against it.\n\n Bounded like ScottyActivity.namespace.\n\n AN EQ (spec.query_field) SINCE 2026-09-25, AND IT DID NOT USED TO BE. The\n reasoning that kept it off was that a boundary is not a caller-supplied\n predicate — right about the CALLER, wrong about the SERVER, and the same\n correction ScottyActivity.namespace already carries. Now that this row\n lives in stratum rather than in a per-tenant kernel collection, the handler\n has to pin the caller's verified claim into the predicate BEFORE the store\n is asked, and a pin needs a criterion to be pinned into; without this line\n the pin is generated and silently ignored. It stays uncallable by clients —\n the c2s surface of a ticket is GetTicket/ListTickets, whose request\n messages carry no namespace field.",
