{
  "name": "ScottyState",
  "package": "scotty",
  "description": "ScottyState IS EMPTY, and that is the whole point of it — the same statement\n profile.ProfileState and work.WorkState make, in the same words, for the same\n reason.\n\n It exists because servicemanager's generated wiring requires every service in\n the build to host one state root, not because this service keeps anything in\n kernel. Jody's 2026-09-25 ruling is explicit — \"kernel is in-memory\n coordination only\", \"something went very wrong if we are holding user data\n like tasks in kernel state\"\n (decisions/kernel-no-durability-user-data-to-stratum-2026-09-25.md item 3) —\n so a field added here later is a CHANGE OF STORAGE PLANE, not a new field.\n\n WHAT WAS HERE, and where it went. Six `repeated` collections — humans,\n policy_versions, tickets, ticket_advice, ticket_decisions, ticket_comments —\n each carrying (spec.persistence) = true, which the same ruling's item 2 turns\n into a generation error (\"durable data belongs in stratum records\"). Every\n one is now a (spec.record) below, read and written through stratum. The dev\n rows that were in kernel are lost on the next full kernel restart and that is\n accepted (ruling item 5).\n\n THE THREE APPEND-ONLY SIDES ARE STILL THREE TYPES, which is the part of this\n move that was NOT mechanical. They were three collections rather than one\n polymorphic entry type because their VISIBILITY differs and that difference\n is the design: §3, \"Joe sees the decision and Dennis's comment. Not the\n advice.\" Three records reached by three readers keep that rule exactly where\n it was — see the (spec.record_read) block at the top of this file for why\n none of them gets a generated client-facing read plane, which is the one way\n the move could have opened a second door onto advice.",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "trace_id",
      "type": "string",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
