{
  "name": "TicketBus",
  "package": "scotty",
  "description": "tacplus:generated EventBus topic wrapper for Ticket.",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "stream",
      "type": "message",
      "message": {
        "name": "Ticket",
        "package": "scotty",
        "description": "Ticket is the IMMUTABLE CORE of a request. §3: \"The ticket is a general\n primitive, not a time-off feature — request, routed, advised, decided,\n commented, immutable, appendable, accounted. It covers overrides, permits and\n approvals generally, and it exercises the entire stack.\"\n\n NO STATUS FIELD, and that is not an omission. \"Immutable\" and \"a lifecycle\n enum this row keeps up to date\" are contradictory, and the lifecycle is\n already fully derivable: a ticket is decided iff a TicketDecision exists for\n it, advised iff a TicketAdvice does. Storing it twice creates a state that\n can disagree with the records it summarises. See the schematic looper's\n decisions/ticket-status-derived-not-stored.md (pre-split snoopy history,\n not in this repo).\n\n NO KIND/CATEGORY FIELD either. §5: \"Prose earns fields once the pattern is\n known; a field never degrades to prose because it got inconvenient.\"\n Time-off, override and permit tickets have not yet shown a common structure,\n so classifying them now would be inventing the taxonomy. request_text carries\n it as attributed prose until the pattern is known. See the schematic\n looper's decisions/ticket-kind-field-deliberately-absent.md (pre-split\n snoopy history, not in this repo).\n\n NO (spec.store_principal), unlike tasks.Task. That option filters reads to\n the row's creating caller, which would make a routed ticket unreadable BY THE\n PERSON IT IS ROUTED TO — the one read the primitive exists to serve.\n\n A stratum record since 2026-09-25, tenant-owned and not `private` — the block\n above `message Human` is the reasoning for all six. The one thing specific to\n Ticket is that its no-(spec.store_principal) has TWO reasons rather than one:\n the company owns the row, AND a ticket has two parties, which is the older\n argument already written just above.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "ticket_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "(spec.indexed) went with the state collection; see Human.human_id.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 64 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 64,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "record_key": true
          },
          {
            "name": "requested_by",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "requested_by is the authenticated PRINCIPAL that opened the ticket, stamped\n from the token by the generated wrapper (see CreateTicketRequest). It is\n the token subject, not a Human id, because it has to be the thing the\n platform can actually vouch for.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "subject_human_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "subject_human_id is the Human the request is ABOUT. Usually the requester\n (\"can I take off early Friday\"), but not necessarily — a foreman opens a\n ticket about a crew member who has no direct access at all, which is\n exactly the unskilled-rolls-up-through-someone case.\n\n Its relationship to requested_by is the open half: the design settles that\n the Human record is the source of truth and the token is ephemeral proof,\n but not how a principal is resolved to a Human id at the seam. See\n decisions/principal-to-human-id-resolution-open.md.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 64 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 64,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "routed_to_human_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "routed_to_human_id is whose decision is being sought — the \"routes to\n Dennis_Scotty\" half of §3. One recipient, not a list: §2's propagation rule\n is \"escalation is exceptional, and ONE LEVEL ONLY. You do not ask your\n boss's boss\", and a repeated field would quietly permit the thing that rule\n forbids.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 64 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 64,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "request_text",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "request_text is the ask, in the requester's own words. It is UNTRUSTED\n INPUT by construction — §5: \"Ticket routing puts one person's text into\n another person's agent's context by design; that is a prompt-injection\n channel created by the feature.\" The two defences are structural and both\n apply here: authority never derives from this string (every authorisation\n in this file is an entitlement or a typed field), and it is attributed at\n the context seam — \"Joe wrote:\", never a directive.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 4000 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 4000,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "opened_at_ms",
            "type": "int64"
          },
          {
            "name": "chain_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "chain_id groups every ticket of ONE negotiation. Jody, 2026-08-30:\n \"the requestor should be able to ask for reconsideration by amending\n request... you want the entire chain.\"\n\n A resubmission after TICKET_DECISION_OUTCOME_AMEND_REQUESTED is a NEW\n ticket row, never a mutation of the old one — this row is immutable and the\n rest of the design is append-only for the reasons\n decisions/ticket-advice-append-vs-single-row.md gives. chain_id is what\n makes the several rows one conversation.\n\n SERVER-ASSIGNED, and the caller cannot supply it: CreateTicketRequest\n carries amends_ticket_id only, and the server copies the predecessor's\n chain_id. Only the FIRST ticket of a chain has chain_id == ticket_id.\n\n Not (spec.indexed): that option marks the COLLECTION KEY, which is\n ticket_id. \"The entire chain\" is ListTickets filtered by this field, the\n same shape routed_to_human_id already has, and it is a service-side filter\n over the collection read — see\n decisions/ticket-decision-chain-2026-08-30.md for the cost.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 64 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 64,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "amends_ticket_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "amends_ticket_id names the IMMEDIATE PREDECESSOR, and is empty for the\n first ticket in a chain. It is kept alongside chain_id rather than instead\n of it because the two answer different questions: this one is \"what did\n this ticket revise\", chain_id is \"what conversation is this\", and reading\n the whole chain off chain_id is one filtered read instead of a walk.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 64 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 64,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "namespace",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "namespace is the row's owning TENANT, and the read half of the isolation\n pair this retrofit adds. Its twin is (spec.namespace) on the Create request\n above: that one binds the field to the caller's namespace claim on the way\n IN (stamp when empty, accept when equal, INVALID_ARGUMENT when different);\n this one is what the generated wrapper filters egress rows against on the\n way OUT, by EXACT string equality — never the level-or-below containment\n the administrative namespace rule uses, because \"users and data always has\n to land in a leaf\".\n\n A row that has to be dropped by that filter ABORTS THE PROCESS rather than\n failing the one call. Reaching it means kernel handed this service another\n tenant's row, which is a leak the filter exists to make impossible, and the\n decision record settles that a crash is the cheaper failure against it.\n\n Bounded like ScottyActivity.namespace.\n\n AN EQ (spec.query_field) SINCE 2026-09-25, AND IT DID NOT USED TO BE. The\n reasoning that kept it off was that a boundary is not a caller-supplied\n predicate — right about the CALLER, wrong about the SERVER, and the same\n correction ScottyActivity.namespace already carries. Now that this row\n lives in stratum rather than in a per-tenant kernel collection, the handler\n has to pin the caller's verified claim into the predicate BEFORE the store\n is asked, and a pin needs a criterion to be pinned into; without this line\n the pin is generated and silently ignored. It stays uncallable by clients —\n the c2s surface of a ticket is GetTicket/ListTickets, whose request\n messages carry no namespace field.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "filter_mode": "eq",
            "store_namespace": true
          },
          {
            "name": "spiffe_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "The publisher, as on Human. Never a read filter; see Human.spiffe_id.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 1024 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1024,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "trace_id",
            "type": "string",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
