{
  "name": "WatchAlertsEnvelope",
  "package": "weather",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "snapshot_item",
      "type": "message",
      "oneof": true,
      "oneof_name": "event",
      "message": {
        "name": "Alert",
        "package": "weather",
        "description": "NWS issues alerts against zones, and a zone covers many places. Storing an\n Alert per place would store the same hurricane watch once per job site in\n Harris County. So the row is keyed by NWS's own alert id and carries the zones\n it names; \"alerts for this place\" is a read that resolves the place's zones\n first (ListAlerts takes either form). Choice E.\n\n Write-once like the rest: an alert NWS UPDATEs or CANCELs arrives as a NEW\n alert row whose references name the one it supersedes. Nothing is edited in\n place, which is also why there is no \"active\" boolean — active is a function\n of (effective, expires, superseded) AT THE MOMENT YOU ASK, not a stored flag\n some poller has to remember to clear.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "alert_id",
            "type": "string",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "record_key": true
          },
          {
            "name": "zone_ids",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "zone_ids are the NWS zones this alert names — forecast zones, county zones,\n or both. Order is NWS's.\n\n A NOTE ON THE REPEATED-STRING BOUND, the same one work.Job carries:\n (spec.assert_max_size) does DOUBLE DUTY on a repeated string field — tacplus\n reads it as the element byte cap AND as the element count, and there is no\n second annotation that separates them. 256 here therefore means \"at most 256\n zone ids, each at most 256 bytes\". A zone id is 6 bytes; 256 zones is a wide\n hurricane watch but not an unheard-of one, and this is the number to revisit\n first if a real alert is ever truncated.",
            "repeated": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 elements",
              "maximum element size: 32768 bytes (default)"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "elements",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "default_max_element_size",
                "target": "element",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 32768,
                "defaulted": true,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "default_bound"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "event",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "event is NWS's event name, verbatim: \"Hurricane Watch\", \"Flash Flood\n Warning\", \"Heat Advisory\". NOT an enum: NWS's list is long, changes, and is\n authoritative elsewhere. A rule in Reveille matches on this string, and\n matching on the bureau's own word is more honest than matching on our lossy\n re-encoding of it. Choice F.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 100 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 100,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "severity",
            "type": "enum",
            "description": "ALL FOUR CARRY (spec.assert_enum_defined), AND THAT ANNOTATION REFUSES THE\n *_UNSPECIFIED ZERO VALUE. It is worth saying at the point of use because the\n consequence is a long way from the declaration: a row stored with one of\n these left at zero is written happily by the state client and then fails\n RESPONSE validation inside the generated wrapper on every read of it,\n including every WATCH frame. That is why the ingest in services/weather/\n src/nws.rs falls back to a DECLARED value for each — UNKNOWN for the first\n three, and ALERT for message_type, which is also CAP's own default for the\n field — rather than leaving an absent value at zero.",
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_enum_defined",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "enum_values": [
