  "description": "WorkState IS EMPTY, and that is the whole point of it — the same statement\n profile.ProfileState makes, in the same words, for the same reason.\n\n It exists because servicemanager's generated wiring requires every service in\n the build to host one state root (the same reason llmproxy.LlmProxyState is\n empty), not because this service keeps anything in kernel. Jody's 2026-09-25\n ruling is explicit — \"kernel is in-memory coordination only\", \"something went\n very wrong if we are holding user data like tasks in kernel state\" — so a\n field added here later is a CHANGE OF STORAGE PLANE, not a new field.\n\n WHAT WAS HERE, and where it went. Six `repeated` collections — jobs, tasks,\n partners, permits, equipment, rfps — each carrying (spec.persistence) = true,\n which the same ruling's item 2 turns into a generation error (\"durable data\n belongs in stratum records\"). Every one is now a (spec.record) below, read\n and written through stratum. The dev rows that were in kernel are lost on the\n next full kernel restart and that is accepted (ruling item 5).\n\n requires_artifact_on_write WENT WITH THEM, and the discipline it named did\n not. That option makes kernel refuse a STATE write with no accompanying\n artifact type; with no state fields left there is no state write for it to\n govern. Every write in services/work/src/service.rs still leaves a\n WorkActivity (design record §1: \"nothing reaches knowledge without leaving an\n artifact\") — it is now the handler's invariant, asserted in\n service_tests.rs, rather than kernel's.",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "trace_id",
      "type": "string",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "noop",
      "type": "bool"
    }
  ]
}

