        "name": "LetsEncryptCert",
        "package": "letsencrypt",
        "description": "LetsEncryptCert is the cluster's Let's Encrypt certificate set, held durably\n by stratum. It is the ONLY durable copy: LetsEncryptState above is a live\n in-memory mirror of it, kept for the gateway's benefit, and kernel is not a\n durability layer (decisions/\n kernel-no-durability-user-data-to-stratum-2026-09-25.md item 4).\n\n The write order is the whole design: obtain or renew -\u003e write THIS record -\u003e\n write LetsEncryptState.cert_data. A crash between the two costs nothing but a\n stale mirror, which the next start rebuilds from here. The reverse order would\n make a mirror that outlives its own durable copy, which is the shape the\n kernel-persistence experiment had.\n\n ONE ROW PER HOSTNAME SET, not one row per issuance: certmagic manages a single\n multi-SAN certificate for Config.hostnames, so a renewal REPLACES this row\n rather than appending beside it (hence MUTABILITY_CRUD, the default). The\n record is not an issuance log and must not become one — the previous\n certificate is of no use to anyone once the new one is live.\n\n THE ACME ACCOUNT TRAVELS WITH IT. certmagic_state below is the whole storage\n tree, account registration and account key included, so the account is durable\n for exactly as long as the certificate it issued is. Splitting the account into\n a record of its own would let the two get out of step, and an account that\n outlives nothing is one more ACME registration against Let's Encrypt's 10 new\n registrations per 3 hours per IP.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "cert_set_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "cert_set_id identifies the hostname set this certificate covers — a stable\n digest of the sorted, comma-joined Config.hostnames list. Derived, never\n configured: two pods reading the same config must compute the same key.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 128 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 128,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "record_key": true
          },
          {
            "name": "tls_cert",
            "type": "message",
            "description": "tls_cert is the certificate chain and private key, in the SAME type\n LetsEncryptCertData.tls_cert holds, so the mirror write is a field copy and\n not a conversion.\n\n NOT assert_required, for the same reason LetsEncryptCertData.tls_cert stopped\n being required in 7ba5060: the FIRST write of this row happens before any\n certificate exists. certmagic registers the ACME account minutes before\n issuance completes, and that account has to become durable at once — Let's\n Encrypt allows 10 new registrations per 3 hours per IP, which a crash-loop\n that re-registers on every boot exhausts. So the account flush writes this\n row with certmagic_state and no tls_cert, and the obtain fills it in. A row\n with no tls_cert means \"the account is durable, the certificate is not yet\".",
            "message": {
              "name": "X509CertChain",
              "package": "x509",
              "description": "X509CertChain contains a certificate chain and optional private key.\n chain[0] is the leaf (end-entity) cert, followed by intermediates, ordered toward root.\n When key is present, the chain is a full credential suitable for TLS.",
              "fields": [
