        "description": "LlmProviderSecret is ONE person's LLM API key, in a record that NO CLIENT-FACING\n RPC RETURNS. That absence is the whole protection, and it is stronger than the\n `(spec.redact)` it replaced: a redaction is a transform that must run on every\n egress path, whereas a type absent from every response message has no egress\n path to run on. Nothing on `service LlmSettings` names this type — check that\n before adding an rpc, because adding one is the only way to undo it.\n\n It is letsencrypt.LetsEncryptCert's posture applied to the same class of\n secret: a cluster TLS private key lives unredacted in a stratum record whose\n readers are named by `s2s_read`, because a service that has to USE a secret\n has to be able to read it, and the grant list is what bounds who else can.\n\n SEPARATE ROW, SAME KEY. Keyed by the same `owner` as LlmProviderSettings, so\n the pair is joinable by identity and there is no second id to keep in step. The\n two are written in one operation and NOT atomically — records have no\n multi-row write — so the handler publishes THIS row first: `api_key_set` on the\n settings row must never claim a key that is not yet durable. The reverse order\n would advertise a key that a crash could lose.",
