{
  "name": "PullAgentCursorResponse",
  "package": "query",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "items",
      "type": "message",
      "repeated": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 1024 elements (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 1024,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "elements",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ],
      "message": {
        "name": "AgentCursor",
        "package": "scottyagent",
        "description": "AgentCursor is one row per user: everything a cycle needs to know about what\n the previous cycle did.\n\n IT WAS KERNEL STATE UNTIL 2026-09-25 AND IS A STRATUM RECORD NOW. The\n departure was deliberate and written up (workers/big8-p1-scotty-agent-\n questions.md §1); the ruling in decisions/kernel-no-durability-user-data-to-\n stratum-2026-09-25.md reverses it, on the ground that kernel holds no durable\n user data. Item 3 of that ruling allows \"a cursor may stay if it is\n rebuildable; the owning looper decides and says why\". THIS CURSOR IS NOT\n ALLOWED TO STAY, and the field that decides it is last_fencing_token below: a\n stale-write reject that does not survive a restart is not a stale-write\n reject. decisions/scottyagent-record-shapes-2026-09-25.md D1.\n\n WHAT THE MOVE COSTS, said here and not only in the decision record, because\n the three reasons the row was kernel state were real and only one of them has\n gone away:\n\n   - VISIBILITY. The design record requires a write to be \"visible to the very\n     next cycle even if it runs moments later on a different instance\". A\n     record reaches stratum through an EventBus publish and an ASYNCHRONOUS\n     ingest, so it is not. The per-user kernel.FencedLock still serializes the\n     read-modify-write; what it can no longer do is make the previous cycle's\n     write readable. This is a real weakening and it is D3 of the decision\n     record — read it before changing anything in `cycle_under_lock`.\n   - THE READ PATH. GONE. `(spec.record_read)` at the top of this file plus\n     the s2s_read grant below generate `sm.agent_cursor_record_reader()`; the\n     shared `query` trait this comment used to describe is no longer how a\n     service reads its own rows.\n   - THE NAMESPACE STAMP. GONE, and the old comment was wrong about the\n     mechanism rather than only out of date. eventbus's PublishHandler is\n     ACCEPT-or-stamp-or-refuse: a row that arrives with (spec.store_namespace)\n     ALREADY SET keeps that value, and only an unset one is stamped from the\n     publisher (decisions/namespace-fix-corrected-2026-09-02.md). So this\n     service writes the DELIVERY's tenant onto the row exactly as it did into\n     kernel state, and the row lands in the user's namespace, not in\n     `/system`. See the hazard note on `namespace` below.\n\n AgentCycle below is the other record and is unchanged: append-shaped, never\n read back by this service, and what the design record's user-facing \"Bird's\n Eye View\" reads through the generated c2s record query plane.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "user",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "user is the principal this cursor belongs to, the RECORD KEY, and the\n ROW'S OWNING PRINCIPAL.\n\n For a message-triggered cycle it is messaging.Message.recipient — the\n ADDRESSEE, not the sender: the loop that wakes is the recipient's agent.\n\n (spec.store_principal) is the same annotation AgentCycle.user carries and\n it does the same job here: the generated egress filter on ListMyAgentState\n drops any row whose `user` is not the caller's own subject, on top of the\n (spec.principal) stamp that already bound the request. Belt and braces by\n construction rather than by a hand-written check, and it is what stands in\n for `private` — see the record block above.\n\n NO (spec.query_field). It would be redundant with the key, which already\n produces the `user` criterion on AgentCursorFilter, and a second criterion\n is exactly what takes stratum's by-key point read away (pointRead fires\n only when the key criterion is the ONLY one set).",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "store_principal": true,
            "record_key": true
          },
          {
            "name": "cycles_completed",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "cycles_completed counts the loop cycles that have finished for this user.\n Monotonic, and the source of AgentCycle.cycle below.",
            "constraints": [
              "must be \u003e= 0"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_ge",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 0,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "last_fencing_token",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "last_fencing_token is THE FENCE, and it is what makes the lock a fencing\n lock rather than a mutex.\n\n A fencing token is kernel's Raft log index of the granting Acquire:\n strictly increasing over every grant of every key, never reused. A resource\n owner stores the highest token it has seen and REJECTS a write carrying a\n lower one — that, not the lock, is what makes a paused or partitioned stale\n holder safe. This field is that stored highest-seen value, and this service\n is the resource owner doing the comparing (see service.rs `write_cursor`).\n\n Without it, a holder that stalled long enough for its lease to lapse could\n wake up and overwrite the cursor a newer holder had already advanced. The\n lock alone narrows that window; it does not close it.",
            "constraints": [
              "must be \u003e= 0"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_ge",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 0,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "last_cycle_at_ms",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "last_cycle_at_ms is when the last completed cycle finished.",
            "constraints": [
              "must be \u003e= 0"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_ge",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 0,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "last_instance_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "last_instance_id is the instance that ran it. Recorded because consecutive\n cycles for one user legitimately run on different instances and \"which pod\n was that\" is otherwise unanswerable after the fact — NOT because anything\n routes on it. Nothing reads this field to make a decision.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "last_wake",
            "type": "message",
            "description": "last_wake is the last event that woke a cycle for this user.",
            "message": {
              "name": "WakeEvent",
              "package": "scottyagent",
              "description": "WakeEvent is ONE thing that woke the loop. A cycle carries every event it\n aggregated, not just the first, so \"why did you wake\" has a complete answer\n even when several arrived at once.",
              "fields": [
                {
                  "name": "reason",
                  "type": "enum",
                  "description": "reason is the trigger kind.",
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_enum_defined",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "bool",
                      "bool_value": true,
                      "source": "annotation"
                    }
                  ],
                  "enum_values": [
                    {
                      "name": "WAKE_REASON_MESSAGE",
                      "number": 1,
                      "description": "A messaging.Message arrived on this user's EventBus queue and this\n instance's consumer group took it."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "source_id",
                  "type": "string",
                  "description": "source_id identifies the specific event. For WAKE_REASON_MESSAGE it is the\n messaging.Message's delivery_id — the record key of the one delivery this\n wake is for, not the message_id it shares with its siblings, because the\n delivery is what this consumer group consumed.",
                  "constraints": [
                    "maximum size: 512 bytes"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_max_size",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 512,
                      "unit": "bytes",
                      "source": "annotation"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "observed_at_ms",
                  "type": "int64",
                  "description": "observed_at_ms is when this instance took the event off the queue.",
                  "constraints": [
                    "must be \u003e= 0"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "assert_ge",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 0,
                      "source": "annotation"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "trace_id",
                  "type": "string",
                  "constraints": [
                    "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
                  ],
                  "constraint_infos": [
                    {
                      "rule": "default_max_size",
                      "target": "field",
                      "value_kind": "int",
                      "int_value": 32768,
                      "defaulted": true,
                      "unit": "bytes",
                      "source": "default_bound"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "name": "noop",
                  "type": "bool"
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "name": "namespace",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "namespace is the row's owning TENANT, by exact string equality against the\n reader's namespace claim.\n\n (spec.store_namespace) here generates the READ-side egress filter on every\n surface that returns this row — including the MCP dispatch, which resolves\n the caller's claim itself, holds each returned row to it, and ABORTS THE\n PROCESS on a row it would otherwise have to drop. That abort is the\n designed behaviour for a genuine tenant violation, not a bug.\n\n WHERE THE VALUE COMES FROM, stated because it is the weak link and hiding\n it would be worse. An event-driven cycle has NO CALLER: nothing\n authenticated this work, so there is no verified claim to stamp from. The\n only tenant signal available is the consumed messaging.Message's own\n `namespace`, and this service copies that. That value is itself stamped by\n eventbus from MESSAGING'S service token rather than from the sender's\n (workers/big8-p1-messaging-questions.md §1, a fleet-wide open gap), so it\n is the right FIELD carrying a value whose provenance is not yet what the\n design intends. Copying it is still correct: it is the only tenant fact the\n event carries, and the moment tacplus grows a delegated s2s publish the\n value becomes the sender's with no change here.\n\n Not a query field: the field exists to BE the egress boundary, a boundary a\n caller can predicate on is not one, and since 2026-09-25 there is a second\n reason — a criterion on it would cost this type its by-key point read. See\n the record block above and D2 of the decision record.\n\n WHAT THE MOVE TO A RECORD CHANGED, AND WHAT IT DID NOT. Nothing about where\n the value comes from: this service still copies the delivery's namespace\n onto the row. eventbus's PublishHandler is ACCEPT-or-stamp-or-refuse — a\n row arriving with this field already set KEEPS that value, and only an\n unset one is stamped from the publishing token\n (decisions/namespace-fix-corrected-2026-09-02.md) — so the row lands in the\n USER'S tenant and the caller's own read of it matches.\n\n THE HAZARD IF THAT EVER STOPS BEING TRUE, because it is a process abort and\n not a dropped row: this service's own namespace is `/system`, so a cursor\n that reached the topic with this field empty would come back stamped\n `/system`, and ListMyAgentState's generated backstop aborts on a row it\n would have to drop. A delivered messaging.Message always carries a\n non-empty namespace (eventbus refuses a publish without one), which is why\n there is no guard in service.rs against a case the platform cannot produce.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "store_namespace": true
          },
          {
            "name": "spiffe_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "spiffe_id is the record owner, and the NAME IS LOAD-BEARING for the same\n reason it is on AgentCycle: eventbus's topic discovery matches the literal\n field name \"spiffe_id\" of string type when it decides which topics get\n publisher-identity stamping, so renaming this field silently turns the\n stamping off. It holds THIS SERVICE's identity, because the publish is s2s;\n `user` above is where the person is.\n\n Left empty by this service on publish. No (spec.query_field): nothing reads\n a cursor by publisher — every read of this type is by key.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 1024 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1024,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "trace_id",
            "type": "string",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "default_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 32768,
                "defaulted": true,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "default_bound"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "noop",
            "type": "bool"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "next_page_token",
      "type": "string",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
