            "description": "blob_id is the platform blob store's id for this document's bytes,\n `documents/\u003cnamespace\u003e/\u003ccontent_hash\u003e` — the same value DocHashIndex.blob_id\n carries for this row's content_hash. It is what makes a document a CONSUMER\n POINTER at a blob rather than a thing that owns bytes (blob store v3 R5: \"a\n consumer's record points at a hash and moves its own pointer\"), and it is\n the one field CreateDocFileLink below needs in order to call\n blobstore.CreateLink.\n\n It comes STRAIGHT FROM blobstore's CreateUploadResponse.blob_id, copied onto\n this row by the same CreateIngest handler that writes the DocHashIndex row\n from the same response (services/docmanager/src/service.rs, write_ingest) —\n NOT read back off the DocHashIndex row at this content_hash. That\n distinction is not a style choice: DocHashIndex has no `s2s_read` grant, so\n tacplus generates no reader over it, and a file_id -\u003e blob_id join through\n it is therefore not expressible today. Copying the value at ingest is the\n only path that exists, and it is safe to copy because a blob_id is derived\n entirely from (group, namespace, content_hash) and blobs are immutable, so\n the two copies cannot drift.\n\n DocHashIndex stays the per-tenant content_hash -\u003e blob_id index for dedup\n lookups; this field is the per-document pointer. Same 1024 bound\n DocHashIndex.blob_id uses, for the same value.\n See decisions/documents-volume-group-2026-09-26.md.",
