        "description": "Ticket is the IMMUTABLE CORE of a request. §3: \"The ticket is a general\n primitive, not a time-off feature — request, routed, advised, decided,\n commented, immutable, appendable, accounted. It covers overrides, permits and\n approvals generally, and it exercises the entire stack.\"\n\n NO STATUS FIELD, and that is not an omission. \"Immutable\" and \"a lifecycle\n enum this row keeps up to date\" are contradictory, and the lifecycle is\n already fully derivable: a ticket is decided iff a TicketDecision exists for\n it, advised iff a TicketAdvice does. Storing it twice creates a state that\n can disagree with the records it summarises. See the schematic looper's\n decisions/ticket-status-derived-not-stored.md (pre-split snoopy history,\n not in this repo).\n\n NO KIND/CATEGORY FIELD either. §5: \"Prose earns fields once the pattern is\n known; a field never degrades to prose because it got inconvenient.\"\n Time-off, override and permit tickets have not yet shown a common structure,\n so classifying them now would be inventing the taxonomy. request_text carries\n it as attributed prose until the pattern is known. See the schematic\n looper's decisions/ticket-kind-field-deliberately-absent.md (pre-split\n snoopy history, not in this repo).\n\n NO (spec.store_principal), unlike tasks.Task. That option filters reads to\n the row's creating caller, which would make a routed ticket unreadable BY THE\n PERSON IT IS ROUTED TO — the one read the primitive exists to serve.\n\n A stratum record since 2026-09-25, tenant-owned and not `private` — the block\n above `message Human` is the reasoning for all six. The one thing specific to\n Ticket is that its no-(spec.store_principal) has TWO reasons rather than one:\n the company owns the row, AND a ticket has two parties, which is the older\n argument already written just above.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "ticket_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "(spec.indexed) went with the state collection; see Human.human_id.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 64 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 64,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "record_key": true
          },
          {
            "name": "requested_by",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "requested_by is the authenticated PRINCIPAL that opened the ticket, stamped\n from the token by the generated wrapper (see CreateTicketRequest). It is\n the token subject, not a Human id, because it has to be the thing the\n platform can actually vouch for.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "subject_human_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "subject_human_id is the Human the request is ABOUT. Usually the requester\n (\"can I take off early Friday\"), but not necessarily — a foreman opens a\n ticket about a crew member who has no direct access at all, which is\n exactly the unskilled-rolls-up-through-someone case.\n\n Its relationship to requested_by is the open half: the design settles that\n the Human record is the source of truth and the token is ephemeral proof,\n but not how a principal is resolved to a Human id at the seam. See\n decisions/principal-to-human-id-resolution-open.md.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 64 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 64,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "routed_to_human_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "routed_to_human_id is whose decision is being sought — the \"routes to\n Dennis_Scotty\" half of §3. One recipient, not a list: §2's propagation rule\n is \"escalation is exceptional, and ONE LEVEL ONLY. You do not ask your\n boss's boss\", and a repeated field would quietly permit the thing that rule\n forbids.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
