          {
            "name": "opened_at_ms",
            "type": "int64"
          },
          {
            "name": "chain_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "chain_id groups every ticket of ONE negotiation. Jody, 2026-08-30:\n \"the requestor should be able to ask for reconsideration by amending\n request... you want the entire chain.\"\n\n A resubmission after TICKET_DECISION_OUTCOME_AMEND_REQUESTED is a NEW\n ticket row, never a mutation of the old one — this row is immutable and the\n rest of the design is append-only for the reasons\n decisions/ticket-advice-append-vs-single-row.md gives. chain_id is what\n makes the several rows one conversation.\n\n SERVER-ASSIGNED, and the caller cannot supply it: CreateTicketRequest\n carries amends_ticket_id only, and the server copies the predecessor's\n chain_id. Only the FIRST ticket of a chain has chain_id == ticket_id.\n\n Not (spec.indexed): that option marks the COLLECTION KEY, which is\n ticket_id. \"The entire chain\" is ListTickets filtered by this field, the\n same shape routed_to_human_id already has, and it is a service-side filter\n over the collection read — see\n decisions/ticket-decision-chain-2026-08-30.md for the cost.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 64 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 64,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "amends_ticket_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "amends_ticket_id names the IMMEDIATE PREDECESSOR, and is empty for the\n first ticket in a chain. It is kept alongside chain_id rather than instead\n of it because the two answer different questions: this one is \"what did\n this ticket revise\", chain_id is \"what conversation is this\", and reading\n the whole chain off chain_id is one filtered read instead of a walk.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 64 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 64,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "namespace",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "namespace is the row's owning TENANT, and the read half of the isolation\n pair this retrofit adds. Its twin is (spec.namespace) on the Create request\n above: that one binds the field to the caller's namespace claim on the way\n IN (stamp when empty, accept when equal, INVALID_ARGUMENT when different);\n this one is what the generated wrapper filters egress rows against on the\n way OUT, by EXACT string equality — never the level-or-below containment\n the administrative namespace rule uses, because \"users and data always has\n to land in a leaf\".\n\n A row that has to be dropped by that filter ABORTS THE PROCESS rather than\n failing the one call. Reaching it means kernel handed this service another\n tenant's row, which is a leak the filter exists to make impossible, and the\n decision record settles that a crash is the cheaper failure against it.\n\n Bounded like ScottyActivity.namespace.\n\n AN EQ (spec.query_field) SINCE 2026-09-25, AND IT DID NOT USED TO BE. The\n reasoning that kept it off was that a boundary is not a caller-supplied\n predicate — right about the CALLER, wrong about the SERVER, and the same\n correction ScottyActivity.namespace already carries. Now that this row\n lives in stratum rather than in a per-tenant kernel collection, the handler\n has to pin the caller's verified claim into the predicate BEFORE the store\n is asked, and a pin needs a criterion to be pinned into; without this line\n the pin is generated and silently ignored. It stays uncallable by clients —\n the c2s surface of a ticket is GetTicket/ListTickets, whose request\n messages carry no namespace field.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
