            "type": "string",
            "description": "tenant_scope is WHERE THIS RULE APPLIES, and it is the whole per-company\n half of Jody's 2026-09-20 amendment (see the service-class note at the top\n of this file). It sits down here, after the platform triple rather than\n beside `effect` where it belongs by meaning, only so the tags stay in\n order: it was added after this message already had 17 of them.\n\n   EMPTY = EVERYWHERE. The rule is the person's own and follows them into\n   every tenant they belong to. This is the default and the common case:\n   \"never interrupt me before 7am\" is not a company's business.\n\n   A TENANT NAMESPACE = ONLY THERE. \"File anything from Dennis under the\n   Muleshoe job\" is a rule about one company, and it must not follow the\n   person out of that company — which is precisely what would happen if a\n   personal profile held it unscoped.\n\n It is a string and not a repeated one: a rule that genuinely applies in two\n named tenants and not a third is two rules, and the person can say so. A\n list here would be a second scoping grammar beside `text` of the kind\n RuleMatch item 4 refuses.\n\n NOT THE SAME FIELD AS `namespace` ABOVE, and the distinction is\n load-bearing: `namespace` is the store's egress backstop, which says who\n may READ the row; this says where the rule has EFFECT once read. A reader\n in tenant B legitimately sees a rule scoped to tenant A — it is their own\n rule, it is in their own list, and it simply does not fire.\n\n NO (spec.query_field), for the same reason nothing inside RuleMatch has\n one. The evaluating runtime wants \"scoped to here OR scoped nowhere\", which\n is a two-value OR that an EQ criterion cannot express; it loads the user's\n bounded rule set and decides in process, as it already does for `match`.",
