types.description:
RpcAnnotation declares an RPC's call surface on two independent axes plus a
 set of non-exclusive protocol flags. It is per-RPC: the axes are properties
 of the call, not of the service that happens to host it.

 Example: option (spec.rpc) = {principal: RPC_PRINCIPAL_SERVICE};
 Example: option (spec.rpc) = {
            principal: RPC_PRINCIPAL_USER,
            exposure: RPC_EXPOSURE_EXTERNAL,
            flags: [RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ENTITLED_USERS]
          };

types.schema_json:
{
  "name": "RpcAnnotation",
  "package": "spec",
  "description": "RpcAnnotation declares an RPC's call surface on two independent axes plus a\n set of non-exclusive protocol flags. It is per-RPC: the axes are properties\n of the call, not of the service that happens to host it.\n\n Example: option (spec.rpc) = {principal: RPC_PRINCIPAL_SERVICE};\n Example: option (spec.rpc) = {\n            principal: RPC_PRINCIPAL_USER,\n            exposure: RPC_EXPOSURE_EXTERNAL,\n            flags: [RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ENTITLED_USERS]\n          };",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "principal",
      "type": "enum",
      "description": "principal is required; RPC_PRINCIPAL_UNSPECIFIED is not a valid declaration.",
      "enum_values": [
        {
          "name": "RPC_PRINCIPAL_USER",
          "number": 1,
          "description": "RPC_PRINCIPAL_USER: the caller acts as a user identity. Internally that is\n a client calling as a given user; externally it is the actual user."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_PRINCIPAL_SERVICE",
          "number": 2,
          "description": "RPC_PRINCIPAL_SERVICE: the caller acts as a service identity."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_PRINCIPAL_MCP",
          "number": 3,
          "description": "RPC_PRINCIPAL_MCP: this RPC is declared as an MCP tool rather than a gRPC\n call. tacplus generates the tool schema and a handler interface method the\n service implements by hand; it maps the tool to no gRPC RPC of its own.\n Being a value of this enum rather than a flag is what keeps a DECLARED tool\n from also being a gRPC call: an rpc block holds one principal.\n\n It is NOT how most tools come to exist. Every user RPC of a service_c2s\n service — not RPC_PRINCIPAL_SERVICE, minus RPC_FLAG_PUBLIC_NO_AUTH and\n RPC_FLAG_SELF_AUTHENTICATES — is published as a tool automatically, with no\n annotation asking for one and with its gRPC call, REST route and\n entitlement grant intact. Declare this principal only for a tool that has\n no gRPC rpc of its own. Authorization is unchanged either way — flags /\n auth_services / target_namespace_field mean the same thing against whatever\n Caller the MCP session captured at handshake as they do against a gRPC\n caller.\n\n Streaming is a hard generation failure on this principal: the tool-call wire\n format has no representation for a stream. A handler is free to drive a\n Watch internally and fold it into one response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "exposure",
      "type": "enum",
      "description": "exposure defaults to RPC_EXPOSURE_INTERNAL.",
      "enum_values": [
        {
          "name": "RPC_EXPOSURE_INTERNAL",
          "number": 0,
          "description": "RPC_EXPOSURE_INTERNAL: reachable only from inside the cluster boundary."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_EXPOSURE_EXTERNAL",
          "number": 1,
          "description": "RPC_EXPOSURE_EXTERNAL: reachable from outside the cluster boundary."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "flags",
      "type": "enum",
      "description": "flags is an unordered set; RPC_FLAG_UNSPECIFIED is not a valid entry.",
      "repeated": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 1024 elements (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 1024,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "elements",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ],
      "enum_values": [
        {
          "name": "RPC_FLAG_PUBLIC_NO_AUTH",
          "number": 1,
          "description": "RPC_FLAG_PUBLIC_NO_AUTH: this RPC is a public endpoint that requires no\n caller token at all (OIDC discovery, JWKS). Nothing authenticates the\n caller, because there is nothing to authenticate."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ANY_USER",
          "number": 2,
          "description": "RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ANY_USER: any authenticated user may call, with no\n entitlement check (e.g. token exchange, event streams)."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ENTITLED_USERS",
          "number": 3,
          "description": "RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ENTITLED_USERS: only users holding a matching entitlement\n may call. This is today's default policy for user-facing RPCs."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_FLAG_SELF_AUTHENTICATES",
          "number": 4,
          "description": "RPC_FLAG_SELF_AUTHENTICATES: this RPC carries no bearer token because it\n authenticates the caller from the request body itself (bootstrap token\n minting, cred-pair JWT). The caller interceptor skips it; the handler does\n the authentication. Distinct from RPC_FLAG_PUBLIC_NO_AUTH: the caller is\n still authenticated, just not by the token path."
        },
        {
          "name": "RPC_FLAG_MINTS_CREDENTIAL",
          "number": 5,
          "description": "RPC_FLAG_MINTS_CREDENTIAL: this RPC's response mints or returns a live\n credential — a token, a certificate, a delegate grant — so it is never\n auto-exposed as an MCP tool, whatever its principal. It carries no\n auth/authorizer semantics of its own: a mint RPC's authorization is\n already fully expressed by its principal, its other flags and its\n auth_services. This flag only affects MCP auto-exposure."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "auth_services",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "auth_services lists the service identities allowed to call this RPC.\n [\"*\"] = any authenticated service. Per-RPC: two RPCs of one service may\n legitimately admit different callers.\n\n On an RPC_PRINCIPAL_USER rpc of a service_c2s service it ADDS the listed\n service identities to the users the rpc already admits: a user token is\n still authorized by its entitlements, a service token by these patterns,\n and a service not listed is refused. This is how one rpc is \"c2s for\n users + s2s for these services\"; the handler's Caller is whichever\n identity the verified token names.",
      "repeated": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 1024 elements (default)",
        "maximum element size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 1024,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "elements",
          "source": "default_bound"
        },
        {
          "rule": "default_max_element_size",
          "target": "element",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "target_namespace_field",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "target_namespace_field names the request field whose string value carries\n the target namespace path (e.g. \"/io/example/tenant\"). When set, the\n authorizer enforces the level-or-below rule: the caller's namespace (from\n the bearer token's `namespace` claim) must equal the target namespace or\n be a strict path-segment ancestor of it. The field must exist on this\n RPC's request message and be of type string.\n\n Use the proto field name (snake_case as declared), e.g. \"tenant_namespace\".\n Leave unset for RPCs whose target is implicit (e.g. List* methods that\n scope the response to the caller's subtree at the handler). Under\n RPC_FLAG_AUTH_ANY_USER this instead holds an absolute namespace literal.",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "mcp_tool_name",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "mcp_tool_name overrides the generated MCP tool name for a\n RPC_PRINCIPAL_MCP rpc. Leave it unset: the default is the RPC name in MCP\n naming convention (snake_case), and the tool description is the rpc's own\n leading // doc comment, so neither is a second surface that can drift from\n the real one. Set it only for the rare RPC whose name does not make a good\n tool name. Must be snake_case ([a-z][a-z0-9_]*), and is only meaningful on\n RPC_PRINCIPAL_MCP — declaring it elsewhere fails generation rather than\n being silently ignored.",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "auth_actors",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "auth_actors lists the delegate identities allowed to call this RPC on\n another principal's behalf. Entries are matched against the token's `act`\n claim — the delegate's service path, the same shape auth_services patterns\n already match — as anchored RE2; [\"*\"] = any actor.\n\n The gate runs before the auth_services / entitlement checks, and only when\n the token actually carries `act`: a token with no `act` claim is authorized\n exactly as it is today. A token that DOES carry `act` is rejected unless\n this RPC declares auth_actors and one of its patterns matches, so an RPC\n that says nothing about delegation admits no delegated call at all.",
      "repeated": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 1024 elements (default)",
        "maximum element size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 1024,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "elements",
          "source": "default_bound"
        },
        {
          "rule": "default_max_element_size",
          "target": "element",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "requires_device",
      "type": "bool",
      "description": "requires_device declares the DEVICE-ONLY APPROVAL check: this RPC is served\n only when the caller presents a pending approval the person consumed with a\n WebAuthn assertion from a device linked to them. A browser IdP session, a\n delegated agent token and a tenant admin all fail it, however completely\n they are the user.\n\n The generated server check reads `x-tacplus-approval-id` and\n `x-tacplus-device-assertion-bin` off the call metadata — never request\n fields, so neither is inside the hashed bytes — computes the canonical hash\n of the request, and calls the authoritative (spec.token_minter) service's\n BrokerPendingApproval. Any failure refuses the RPC: the handler is never\n reached. The S2S client for that call is auto-injected into the declaring\n package; the author writes only this boolean.\n\n The proposing half (creating the pending approval) is the caller's own\n call, not generated. The caller-side generated helper computes the request\n hash with the SAME canonicalization the check uses and attaches the two\n metadata keys."
    },
    {
      "name": "requires_device_when",
      "type": "message",
      "description": "requires_device_when narrows the same DEVICE-ONLY APPROVAL check above to\n the requests where ONE named request field holds ONE named value. It exists\n for the unified-CRUD shape: an rpc that writes three kinds of row, only one\n of which is a decision about another person, where marking the whole rpc\n would gate the two that are not.\n\n It is mutually exclusive with requires_device: a conditional gate is a\n narrower requires_device, not an addition to it. Everything else is\n identical — when the condition holds, the SAME check runs over the SAME\n canonical hash of the WHOLE request, so every structural precondition\n (verified caller, plain unary rpc, no map field in the request tree) still\n applies to the whole rpc, unconditionally.",
      "message": {
        "name": "RequiresDeviceWhen",
        "package": "spec",
        "description": "RequiresDeviceWhen is the condition (spec.rpc).requires_device_when gates on.\n Both halves are resolved at generation time against the rpc's request\n descriptor: an unknown field, a field that is not a plain string or enum, or\n an enum value name the enum does not have all fail generation rather than\n becoming a comparison that is silently never true.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "field",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "field is a top-level field name on the RPC's request message. It must be a\n plain (non-repeated, non-map, no explicit presence) string or enum field.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "default_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 32768,
                "defaulted": true,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "default_bound"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "equals",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "equals is the value that field must hold for the gate to apply — the enum\n value's short name (e.g. \"RULE_EFFECT_DELEGATION\") for an enum field, or\n the literal string for a string field.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "default_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 32768,
                "defaulted": true,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "default_bound"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "name": "ServiceAnnotation",
  "package": "spec",
  "description": "ServiceAnnotation declares the owning service identity for a proto file.",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "name",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "name is the canonical service name (e.g. \"identity\", \"gateway\").",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "store",
      "type": "bool",
      "description": "store declares this service as a backing store.\n Other services reference it via StateAnnotation.label."
    },
    {
      "name": "config_store",
      "type": "bool",
      "description": "config_store declares this service as THE backing store for auto-injected\n per-service config state (the \u003cService\u003eConfigState messages tacplus injects\n from (spec.grpc_config)). At most one service in a proto set may set it.\n Distinct from store: store says \"this service can back state\", config_store\n says which one config is routed to. Without it, a proto set with more than\n one store has no non-arbitrary answer."
    },
    {
      "name": "gateway",
      "type": "bool",
      "description": "gateway declares this service as a gateway (reverse proxy).\n Changes TLS source to GatewayTlsState (public cert) instead of X509Mint (internal CA).\n The generated run() watches GatewayTlsState for cert hot-reload instead of running\n a TLS renewal loop."
    },
    {
      "name": "rest_dispatch",
      "type": "bool",
      "description": "rest_dispatch generates REST→gRPC dispatch code for all C2S RPCs visible\n to this service. The service acts as a reverse proxy, translating HTTP/JSON\n requests into typed gRPC calls. Used by gateway and the CLI."
    },
    {
      "name": "accepted_audiences",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "accepted_audiences widens the set of token `aud` values this service's\n generated Authorizer accepts, beyond the two it already derives: its own\n service label, plus every vservice/trait it hosts (AcceptedAudiencesForHost).\n Omitting it — which every service does today — leaves that derived set\n exactly as it is, so this field is backward compatible by construction.\n\n Declaring an audience here does NOT grant anything. It only stops the\n Authorizer rejecting a token that was correctly minted for that audience;\n the caller must still be entitled to the RPC. Who may add an entry is not a\n runtime question: this is a proto annotation under VCS and code review, the\n same trust boundary as (spec.trait) or an entitlement declaration.",
      "repeated": true,
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 1024 elements (default)",
        "maximum element size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 1024,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "elements",
          "source": "default_bound"
        },
        {
          "rule": "default_max_element_size",
          "target": "element",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "tenant_owned",
      "type": "bool",
      "description": "tenant_owned declares this service as a per-tenant instance: the operator\n runs one deployment per tenant, and the service's SPIFFE identity is\n spiffe://\u003ctd\u003e/tenants/\u003ctenant\u003e/services/\u003cname\u003e instead of the shared\n /system/services/\u003cname\u003e every other service gets. Tenant identity comes\n ONLY from the caller's token namespace at runtime — this annotation does\n not carry a tenant, it only declares that the service is instantiated\n per-tenant. Read by the operator (per-tenant Deployments), the gateway\n (route by token namespace), and the record-plane store wrapper (tenant\n pin on S2S reads). Default false — every service today is shared."
    },
    {
      "name": "pinned_tenant",
      "type": "string",
      "description": "pinned_tenant pins a tenant-owned service to exactly one tenant. Meaningful\n ONLY alongside tenant_owned = true: when set, the operator instantiates\n exactly one instance of the service, in that tenant, and does NOT loop over\n the configured tenants. Identity is unchanged — the instance is still\n spiffe://\u003ctd\u003e/tenants/\u003cpinned_tenant\u003e/services/\u003cname\u003e; only the cardinality\n changes. This is for a fleet-singleton service that lives in a reserved\n tenant (one row per person, not one row per company), where per-tenant\n replication would split the rows the service exists to hold. The operator\n FAILS reconciliation if the named tenant is not configured: a pin to a\n tenant that does not exist is a config error, not an instance to skip\n silently. Empty — the default, and what every service today carries —\n leaves tenant_owned's per-tenant replication exactly as it is.",
