        "description": "Human is the durable identity record.\n\n The load-bearing principle, from architecture-sketch.md §4 and reaffirmed by\n the design record: \"the Human record is the durable source of truth;\n OAuth/SAML claims are ephemeral proof of 'this session is really this\n person', never themselves the store of who someone is or what they're\n allowed to do.\"\n\n DELIBERATELY ABSENT — every one of these appears in the architecture sketch's\n draft and is contradicted or unsettled by the later design record:\n   - org_unit_id / reports_through: §2 \"Hierarchy\" says these as single\n     strings \"do not express this\", because a chain can differ per job.\n   - rule_auth_level: \"rule\" was retired in favour of \"written policy\", and\n     the sketch derived this field FROM org_unit_id, which is the field above.\n   - personal_scotty_id: agent-instance binding is not settled as a type.\n See the schematic looper's decisions/human-hierarchy-fields-omitted-not-invented.md\n (pre-split snoopy history, not in this repo).\n\n Human carries NO (spec.store_principal). On tasks.Task that option filters\n every read down to the creating caller — correct for a personal task list and\n wrong here, because a Human record exists precisely to be read by people\n other than whoever typed it in. Read authorization for Human is entitlement\n work and is not done; see the schematic looper's\n decisions/scotty-read-authorization-not-wired.md (pre-split snoopy history,\n not in this repo).\n\n A STRATUM RECORD SINCE 2026-09-25, AND TENANT-OWNED RATHER THAN `private`.\n Human is the first of the six, so the reasoning that applies to all of them\n lives here and the other five point at it.\n\n Item 3 of the ruling says \"private where the data is one person's\". NONE of\n these six is one person's, and Human is the clearest case: the file header\n above already says it carries no (spec.store_principal) precisely because\n \"a Human record exists to be read by people other than whoever typed it in\".\n A private record is additionally REQUIRED to carry (spec.store_principal)\n (ValidateRecordPrivateAnnotations) and is REFUSED an s2s_subscribe, which\n would leave these rows with no declared ingest path into stratum at all —\n the gap that has `profile` unable to read its own rows today\n (decisions/profile-private-record-read-gap-2026-09-23.md). So `private` is\n not merely wrong for the data, it is unbuildable for it.\n\n WHAT KEEPS TENANTS APART IS `namespace`, AND IT IS BOTH HALVES OF THAT JOB.\n (spec.store_namespace) is mandatory on a record (ValidateRecordNamespaceAnnotations)\n and drives the generated wrapper's egress filter; the EQ (spec.query_field)\n beside it is what lets a criterion be PINNED into the predicate before the\n store is asked. The query_field is the half that is easy to forget: the store\n evaluates only the criteria a record annotates, so an unannotated namespace\n field yields a filter that is present, generated and inert, leaving the\n aborting egress backstop as the only boundary. It matters MORE than it did in\n kernel: kernel state was chrooted per tenant, while (spec.record).s2s_read's\n own doctrine is that \"an authorized peer service reads FLEET-WIDE, across\n every tenant's rows\". So the pin is not belt-and-braces here, it IS the\n tenant boundary, and services/scotty/src/service.rs applies it on every read\n path from the caller's verified claim.\n\n NO OTHER (spec.query_field) ON ANY OF THE SIX, deliberately — `work`'s D4\n rule, and it holds here for a reason of scotty's own. Every filter these rpcs\n offer (a policy_id, a chain_id, a routed_to_human_id, a ticket_id, the\n participant gate, the requester predicate) is applied by the handler over the\n tenant's rows, which is exactly what it did when they came from kernel's\n fetch_all. A ticket_id pushdown onto the three appended sides looks tempting\n and is the one that would HURT: `count_chain_hops` counts the advice and\n comments of every ticket sharing a chain_id, so pinning ticket_id would turn\n one scan into one read per ticket in the chain, on the write path of every\n create. When a collection gets big enough that the tenant scan is the\n problem, the pushdown is a measured change with a number behind it, not a\n guess made here.",
