        "description": "One customer, any number of subs (Jody, 2026-09-09). A real place. The crew\n on it. Permits and papers hang off it by id. The job's chat is a Chat of\n kind JOB in the chat plane, referenced here, never duplicated.\n\n A NOTE ON THE REPEATED-STRING BOUNDS BELOW. (spec.assert_max_size) bounds\n the element COUNT; (spec.assert_element_max_size) bounds each element's\n BYTE LENGTH. Each of the three repeated id lists here is bounded at 64 ids\n of at most 64 bytes each, which is exactly the width of every *_id key in\n this file. That is also what keeps one Job inside the Tier-1 inline budget\n the MCP job list needs; see ListMyJobs.\n THE SIX KNOWLEDGE RECORDS ARE TENANT-OWNED, NOT `private`, AND THIS IS THE\n ONE PLACE THAT DISTINCTION IS WRITTEN DOWN. Job is the first of them, so the\n reasoning lives here and the other five point at it.\n\n Every record in this fleet before 2026-09-25 was one of two shapes, and these\n six are the third:\n\n   - WorkActivity / ScottyActivity / AgentCycle: rows with an OWNING PERSON.\n     Each carries (spec.store_principal) on the field naming that person, and\n     the c2s read plane pins a caller to their own rows at C2S_SCOPE_PRINCIPAL.\n   - profile.UserProfile / profile.RuleNode: the same, plus (spec.record).\n     private — \"a user's data and nobody else's, ever\"\n     (decisions/identity-people-tenant-devices-private-2026-09-21.md §4).\n   - THESE: rows with no owning person at all. A job belongs to the COMPANY.\n     So does a customer, a permit, the C88, a bid. The design record's own\n     first line says it — \"these rows belong to the company, not to a person,\n     and exist whether or not anyone talks to Scotty\" — and\n     decisions/profile-service-proto-design-2026-09-20.md draws the same line\n     from the other side when it puts a person's preferences in a PERSONAL\n     service precisely because work's rows are not personal.\n\n So NO (spec.store_principal) ON ANY OF THE SIX, and that is not the omission\n it looks like. The annotation declares \"the one principal whose rows these\n are\", and naming one here would be a false statement with a real consequence:\n the generated egress row filter would drop every job not created by the\n reader, so a foreman would stop seeing the jobs the office opened. `created_by`\n is provenance, not ownership, and it is left as a plain string for that reason.\n (The same argument Task.assigned_to already makes for a different reason — two\n principals rather than none — and it is why the task participant gate is in the\n handler.)\n\n NOT `private` EITHER, for the reverse of profile's reason:\n ValidateRecordPrivateAnnotations requires a private record to carry\n (spec.store_principal), which these have nothing truthful to put in, and it\n refuses s2s_subscribe on one, which would leave these rows with no declared\n ingest path into stratum at all (the unresolved gap\n decisions/profile-people-tenant-single-instance-2026-09-21.md §Gap 2 records,\n and the reason profile cannot read its own rows today —\n decisions/profile-private-record-read-gap-2026-09-23.md). A tenant-scoped\n record has no such gap: it subscribes, it is read over the plane's S2S half,\n and its boundary is the tenant.\n\n WHAT KEEPS TENANTS APART IS `namespace`, AND IT IS BOTH HALVES OF THAT JOB.\n (spec.store_namespace) is mandatory on a record (ValidateRecordNamespaceAnnotations)\n and drives the generated wrapper's egress filter; the EQ (spec.query_field)\n added to it here is what lets a criterion be PINNED into the predicate before\n the store is asked. Both are needed, and the query_field is the half that is\n easy to forget: the store evaluates only the criteria a record annotates, so\n an unannotated namespace field yields a filter that is \"present, generated,\n and inert\" (record_read_validate.go), leaving the crashing egress backstop as\n the only boundary. It matters more on the S2S half than it did in kernel:\n (spec.record).s2s_read's own cross-tenant doctrine is that \"an authorized peer\n service reads FLEET-WIDE, across every tenant's rows\", where the kernel state\n this replaced was chrooted per tenant. So the pin is not belt-and-braces here,\n it IS the tenant boundary, and services/work/src/service.rs applies it on every\n read path from the caller's verified claim.\n\n NO OTHER (spec.query_field) ON THESE SIX, deliberately. Every filter the\n Get/List rpcs offer — a status, a customer, membership of a repeated sub or\n crew list, a lapsing horizon, the two-principal task gate — is applied by the\n handler over the tenant's rows, which is exactly what it did when those rows\n came from kernel's fetch_all. Declaring criteria for the subset that happens\n to be expressible would push half of one read into the store and leave the\n other half in the handler, for no behaviour change; when a collection gets\n big enough that the tenant scan is the problem, the pushdown is a measured\n change with a number behind it, not a guess made here.",
