  "name": "MailboxEntryWatchChange",
  "package": "query",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "type",
      "type": "enum",
      "enum_values": [
        {
          "name": "RECORD_CHANGE_TYPE_ADD",
          "number": 1
        },
        {
          "name": "RECORD_CHANGE_TYPE_MODIFY",
          "number": 2
        },
        {
          "name": "RECORD_CHANGE_TYPE_DELETE",
          "number": 3
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "item",
      "type": "message",
      "message": {
        "name": "MailboxEntry",
        "package": "mailbox",
        "description": "MailboxEntry is ONE DELIVERY'S mailbox state, keyed by the SAME delivery_id\n as the `messaging.Message` it tracks.\n\n Joinable by identity, not by a second lookup: an entry and its message share\n a key, so \"fetch the body of the mail I was just told about\" is one Message\n read with an id the client already holds.\n\n IT HOLDS NO BODY. `messaging.Message.text` is never copied here — that is the\n decision doc's rejected alternative (\"Mailbox stores full message content\"),\n and it stands. What IS carried is the inbox SUMMARY LINE the doc's own\n ListHeldMail spec enumerates (sender, subject, sent_at_ms): a mail client\n renders a list before anything is opened, and those three fields are the only\n way to serve that list without a second round trip per row. A summary line is\n not a second copy of the message, and the field that would make it one —\n `text` — is absent, as are `recipients` and `ticket_id`.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "delivery_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "delivery_id is the record key and messaging.Message.delivery_id verbatim.\n Bounded identically (512) — a shorter bound here would silently drop the\n deliveries whose recipient subject is long.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 512 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 512,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_min_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "record_key": true
          },
          {
            "name": "message_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "message_id groups the N entries of one submission, the same way it groups\n the N Messages. EQ-filterable for the same reason it is there.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 128 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 128,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "filter_mode": "eq"
          },
          {
            "name": "recipient",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "recipient is whose mailbox this is, and the ROW'S OWNING PRINCIPAL — the\n same field, carrying the same annotation, as messaging.Message.recipient.\n The generated egress filter holds a caller's reads to their own rows, so\n ListHeldMail cannot return one person's mail to another even if this\n service's own predicate were wrong.\n\n spiffe_id below is the record's declared owner but is the PUBLISHER —\n mailbox's own service identity, stamped by eventbus — so it is the wrong\n field to filter a user's reads on, exactly as it is on Message.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              },
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "filter_mode": "eq",
            "store_principal": true
          },
          {
            "name": "status",
            "type": "enum",
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_enum_defined",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "bool",
                "bool_value": true,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "enum_values": [
              {
                "name": "MAILBOX_ENTRY_STATUS_HELD",
                "number": 1,
                "description": "HELD: the delivery arrived on the topic and nobody has pulled it. Age alone\n does not change that, and no longer needs a marker to say so: an unpulled\n row may leave the store's MEMORY like any other and loses nothing by it —\n it stays complete and queryable on disk, and the next read reloads it.\n NOTHING removes a HELD row: the 90-day `held_since_ms` floor that used to\n was the kernel state root's bound, and the root went away with it on\n 2026-09-27 — see Config.sweep_interval_seconds."
              },
              {
                "name": "MAILBOX_ENTRY_STATUS_RETRIEVED",
                "number": 2,
                "description": "RETRIEVED: the recipient acknowledged pulling it. Terminal in the normal\n path — `ListHeldMail` stops returning the row and `UpdateHeldMail` is a\n no-op on it — so this is the row's last write, and therefore where its\n residency idle clock starts running down."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "held_since_ms",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "held_since_ms is when this consumer group's copy landed. It is NOT a\n residency clock — residency runs on the row's last touch, which is\n store_epoch_ms and is the store's business, not a business field's. It is\n not an expiry clock either, as of 2026-09-27: the state-root sweep it used\n to drive is gone with the state root, and no record-side clock replaced it\n (see Config.sweep_interval_seconds). What survives is the question a user\n asks with it — \"how long has this been sitting in my mailbox\" — and the\n ordering `ListHeldMail` presents.\n\n RANGE-filterable because \"what has been sitting in my mailbox since last\n week\" is a question about this field.",
            "constraints": [
              "must be \u003e= 0"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_ge",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 0,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "filter_mode": "range"
          },
          {
            "name": "retrieved_at_ms",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "retrieved_at_ms is unset until retrieved: when the recipient took it, which\n is what a read inbox sorts by and what UpdateHeldMail's idempotency is\n stated in terms of (the FIRST retrieval's timestamp stands).\n\n It gates nothing in this service any more. Until 2026-09-20 it was the\n clock the eviction-eligibility mark was measured from; that mark went with\n `ejectionable`, and the residency clock that replaced it counts from the\n row's last WRITE — which, for a retrieved row, is the retrieval itself. The\n field survives because \"when did I take this\" is the user's question and\n does not stop being worth answering.",
            "constraints": [
              "must be \u003e= 0"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_ge",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 0,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "sender",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "sender is messaging.Message.sender — the authenticated principal that sent\n it, never a caller-written string on either side.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "filter_mode": "eq"
          },
          {
            "name": "subject",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "subject is the subject line. Bounded identically to Message.subject.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 512 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 512,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "sent_at_ms",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "sent_at_ms is the sender's clock, carried through so an inbox can sort by\n when a message was SENT rather than by when this consumer group happened to\n process it. The two differ by broker latency and by any replay.",
            "constraints": [
              "must be \u003e= 0"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_ge",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 0,
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "store_epoch_ms",
            "type": "int64",
            "description": "store_epoch_ms is the row's LAST WRITE, stamped by the generated wrapper\n and never by this service. It is the record's durable write clock: what the\n disk leg ranges on, and what `residency.deleted_max_age_seconds` would\n measure a delete from if this type had a (spec.deleted_flag). The memory\n idle clock also counts READS, which no field can see — which is precisely\n why residency is a property of the type and not of the row.\n\n RANGE per (spec.store_epoch)'s own contract — it is the predicate a\n two-leg memory/disk query partitions on.",
            "filter_mode": "range"
          },
          {
            "name": "spiffe_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "spiffe_id is the record owner, and the name is LOAD-BEARING: eventbus's\n topic discovery matches the literal field name \"spiffe_id\" of string type\n when deciding which topics get publisher-identity stamping.\n\n It holds MAILBOX'S OWN service identity — this service is the publisher.\n `recipient` above is where the person is.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 1024 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 1024,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "filter_mode": "eq"
          },
          {
            "name": "namespace",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "namespace is the row's owning TENANT, by EXACT string equality against the\n caller's namespace claim. MANDATORY: tacplus fails generation for any\n (spec.record) declaring no such field, on the reasoning that the record\n nobody remembered to annotate is exactly the one that leaks.\n\n Its value is copied from the Message that produced this entry rather than\n resolved again: the tenant of a mailbox entry IS the tenant of the delivery\n it tracks, and re-deriving it from mailbox's own service token would stamp\n the SERVICE's tenant onto a USER's row.\n\n Not a (spec.query_field): the field exists to be the egress boundary, and a\n boundary a caller can name in a predicate is not one.",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 256 bytes"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 256,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "annotation"
              }
            ],
            "store_namespace": true
          },
          {
            "name": "trace_id",
            "type": "string",
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "default_max_size",
                "target": "field",
                "value_kind": "int",
                "int_value": 32768,
                "defaulted": true,
                "unit": "bytes",
                "source": "default_bound"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "noop",
            "type": "bool"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "revision",
      "type": "int64"
    },
    {
      "name": "trace_id",
      "type": "string",
      "constraints": [
        "maximum size: 32768 bytes (default)"
      ],
      "constraint_infos": [
        {
          "rule": "default_max_size",
          "target": "field",
          "value_kind": "int",
          "int_value": 32768,
          "defaulted": true,
          "unit": "bytes",
          "source": "default_bound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "noop",
      "type": "bool"
    }
  ]
}

