{
  "name": "PlaceWatchChange",
  "package": "query",
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "type",
      "type": "enum",
      "enum_values": [
        {
          "name": "RECORD_CHANGE_TYPE_ADD",
          "number": 1
        },
        {
          "name": "RECORD_CHANGE_TYPE_MODIFY",
          "number": 2
        },
        {
          "name": "RECORD_CHANGE_TYPE_DELETE",
          "number": 3
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "item",
      "type": "message",
      "message": {
        "name": "Place",
        "package": "weather",
        "description": "===========================================================================\n THE FOUR RECORDS HAVE NO TENANT, AND THIS IS THE ONE PLACE THAT IS WRITTEN\n DOWN. Place is the first of them, so the reasoning lives here and the other\n three point at it.\n\n Every record in this fleet before this file was one of three shapes:\n\n   - rows with an OWNING PERSON (work.WorkActivity, scotty.ScottyActivity,\n     scottypersonal.ChatSession): (spec.store_principal) on the field naming\n     that person, and the c2s plane pins a caller to their own rows;\n   - the same, plus (spec.record).private (profile.UserProfile) — \"a user's\n     data and nobody else's, ever\";\n   - rows with no owning person but an OWNING TENANT (work.Job, scotty.Ticket,\n     scottypersonal.Chat): no principal, and `namespace` IS the boundary.\n\n THESE FOUR ARE THE FOURTH SHAPE: rows with NO owner of any kind. A place is a\n cell in NWS's grid. An observation is what the sky did there. Neither belongs\n to a person or to a company, both are DEDUPLICATED ACROSS THE FLEET by\n construction (choice A: place_id is DERIVED from the resolved cell, so two\n tenants 400m apart write the same key), and the whole reason this service is\n 9a — one instance for the whole fleet — is that a per-tenant copy would be N\n copies of one fact and N callers hammering api.weather.gov.\n\n SO NO (spec.store_principal) ON ANY OF THE FOUR. There is no field that could\n truthfully carry one; `weather` has never had a (spec.principal) anywhere and\n the reads take no caller into account at all.\n\n NOT `private` EITHER, and here it is not even close. These are PUBLIC FACTS.\n ValidateRecordPrivateAnnotations additionally requires a private record to\n carry (spec.store_principal) — which there is nothing to put in — and refuses\n s2s_subscribe on one, which would leave these rows with no declared ingest\n path into stratum at all. Item 3's \"private where the data is one person's\"\n does not fire for this service in either direction.\n\n AND `namespace` IS DECLARED, EMPTY ON EVERY ROW, AND IS NOT A QUERY\n CRITERION. That is the sentence a reviewer should push back on, so here is the\n whole of it.\n\n   (spec.store_namespace) is MANDATORY on a record —\n   ValidateRecordNamespaceAnnotations fails generation without it, on the\n   reasoning that \"the record nobody remembered to annotate is exactly the one\n   that leaks\". So the field exists here because the platform insists a record\n   name its owning tenant, and this record HAS no owning tenant.\n\n   THE VALUE IS `/system` ON EVERY ROW, FOREVER, and it is derived rather than\n   invented. Kernel mints this service's subject as\n   `/system/services/weather` (SpiffeIds.build with an empty namespace is \"the\n   shared /system root\"; a tenant-owned instance would get\n   `/tenants/\u003ct\u003e/services/\u003csvc\u003e` instead), and the platform's own\n   `namespace_from_spiffe` reduces that subject to the namespace `/system`. So\n   `/system` IS the namespace of the identity that owns these rows, computed by\n   the platform's rule — which is the most truthful thing the field can hold for\n   a 9a service, and reads as what it is: the platform owns this row, no tenant\n   does. `spiffe_id` below carries the full identity; this carries its\n   namespace, which is exactly the relationship the two annotations describe\n   everywhere else.\n\n   THE EMPTY STRING WAS THE OTHER CANDIDATE AND IS STRICTLY WORSE. It would say\n   \"no tenant\" just as well, but it is indistinguishable from the field nobody\n   filled in — the failure ValidateRecordNamespaceAnnotations exists to prevent\n   — and, decisively, it would make the WATCH rpcs ABORT THIS SERVICE on every\n   frame. See the hazard note on the field itself.\n\n   AND IT CARRIES NO (spec.query_field), SO NO READ PINS IT. This is where this\n   file departs from `work` and `scotty`, which both added an EQ criterion and\n   pin it on every read. There, the pin IS the tenant boundary: the record\n   plane's S2S half reads FLEET-WIDE by declared doctrine, so without a pinned\n   criterion one tenant's handler would read another's rows. Here every row in\n   the type carries the same value, so a criterion on it cannot narrow\n   ANYTHING — and it is not free: the record store's by-key fast path\n   (RecordMapImplGenerator.pointRead) fires only when the key criterion is the\n   ONLY criterion set, so pinning an always-true namespace would turn every\n   point read in this service — including the poller's write-once check, once\n   per row per pass over up to ten million of them — into a predicate scan. A\n   criterion that narrows nothing and costs the point read is not a boundary,\n   it is overhead wearing a boundary's clothes.\n\n   WHAT IS LOST IS NOTHING THAT EXISTED. The no-(spec.principal)/-(spec.namespace)\n   note on the `Weather` service block already says it out loud: \"`weather` has\n   NO GENERATED TENANT BOUNDARY ON ANY SURFACE. That is correct for public rows.\n   The day a tenant-specific field lands on one of these rows, nothing at build\n   time or runtime will catch it.\" That was true when these were kernel\n   collections and it is true now; the storage move neither added the exposure\n   nor closed it. WHAT HAS CHANGED, and it is a real narrowing, is that a\n   kernel collection was chrooted per tenant and a record store is one\n   fleet-wide set of rows — which for these four types is not a loss, because\n   fleet-wide is what a deduplicated public fact wants and what the old code\n   already asked kernel for.\n\n THE PUSHDOWNS ARE REAL HERE, unlike in `work`. work's D4 declared no\n (spec.query_field) beyond the tenant, on the grounds that half a read in the\n store and half in the handler is no behaviour change over a tenant's rows.\n That argument does not survive the absence of a tenant: with nothing pinned,\n \"every row of the type\" is up to ten million observations, and a handler-side\n filter over them is a paged gRPC walk of the whole store on every ListObservations.\n So the criteria that are DECLARED PARAMETERS of a read rpc AND actually narrow\n are pushed down — place_id and the time window on Observation and Forecast, the\n time window on Alert — and nothing else is. What stays in the handler is named\n per record below.\n ===========================================================================",
