        }
      ],
      "message": {
        "name": "Alert",
        "package": "weather",
        "description": "NWS issues alerts against zones, and a zone covers many places. Storing an\n Alert per place would store the same hurricane watch once per job site in\n Harris County. So the row is keyed by NWS's own alert id and carries the zones\n it names; \"alerts for this place\" is a read that resolves the place's zones\n first (ListAlerts takes either form). Choice E.\n\n Write-once like the rest: an alert NWS UPDATEs or CANCELs arrives as a NEW\n alert row whose references name the one it supersedes. Nothing is edited in\n place, which is also why there is no \"active\" boolean — active is a function\n of (effective, expires, superseded) AT THE MOMENT YOU ASK, not a stored flag\n some poller has to remember to clear.",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "alert_id",
            "type": "string",
            "required": true,
