        "package": "blobstore",
        "description": "UploadSession is one upload's durable reservation and audit trail (R9).\n\n IT IS THE AUTHORIZATION FOR THE PUT. The upload token carries nothing: it is\n raw randomness, and this row — keyed by its sha256 — is where the upload's\n terms live. A pod receiving a PUT hashes the token, reads this row, and takes\n the group, namespace, declared hash, size and content type from it. Exactly\n the shape BlobLink gives a `/b/` token (R3, R7); there is no signing key and\n nothing to rotate.\n\n THE COST, STATED: a record reaches stratum on an eventbus publish, which is\n enqueue-and-retry, so a PUT arriving milliseconds after CreateUpload returned\n can find this row missing and be refused. BlobLink has had exactly that race\n since it was written and it is accepted there for the same reason — a caller\n that just asked for the token retries.\n\n This row is ALSO the quota reservation, the expiry sweep that releases it, the\n single-use ledger (state leaves PENDING once), and the answer to \"what was\n uploaded, by whom, when\".",
        "fields": [
          {
            "name": "upload_id",
            "type": "string",
            "description": "upload_id is the record key: the lowercase hex SHA-256 of the raw upload\n token, exactly as BlobLink.token_hash is for a `/b/` token. A PUT hashes\n what arrived in the path and looks up THAT, so the key is not a secret even\n though the path is. It is derived from the token and not from the content\n hash: two tenants uploading the same bytes at the same time are two\n sessions.",
            "required": true,
            "constraints": [
              "maximum size: 128 bytes",
              "minimum size: 1"
            ],
            "constraint_infos": [
              {
                "rule": "assert_required",
